{
  "entity1": {
    "rationale": "This is the final iteration, so the package has to be the strongest design I can justify. The best measured design is iteration 10 (556.7): five sensors at x = ±0.04 m, y = 0.06 m, 12 mm footprints, wheel_track 0.095 m, mass 0.45 kg, kp 3.5, kd 0.6, ki 0.2, base_speed 1.4, last_direction line loss. Mass is the only lever that has moved the s-bend in the right direction since iteration 5. Going 0.60 to 0.45 kg lifted the score from 465.8 to 556.7, and s-bend finish rose from 0.20 to 0.55. Its s-bend steering oscillation also fell (0.377 to 0.360), so jitter is not rising with lower mass. Lower mass reduces yaw inertia, mass*(L^2+W^2)/12, which fits the remaining s-bend off-track exits. I accept the Builder's 0.40 kg (the contract minimum). The step is small and the trend has held. The geometry is copied exactly from the Builder's proposal. I keep the iteration 10 controller unchanged (kp 3.5, kd 0.6, ki 0.2, base_speed 1.4, weights [-2,-1,0,1,2], last_direction), so any change in the race is attributable to mass alone. Expected result (a prediction, not a measurement): oval finish stays at 1.00, s-bend max_error at or below 0.05 m, and a total score near or above 556.7. The main risk is slip or traction loss at the lowest mass, or extra s-bend jitter. Those would appear as falling oval finish or s-bend steering_oscillation above about 0.43. Because this is the last iteration, I cannot revert after the race, so this is a deliberate trade: the measured trend supports the lighter robot, and the unseen track is judged on the design delivered here.",
    "geometry": {
      "body_length": 0.18,
      "body_width": 0.12,
      "mass": 0.4,
      "sensor_positions": [
        {"x": -0.04, "y": 0.06},
        {"x": -0.02, "y": 0.06},
        {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.06},
        {"x": 0.02, "y": 0.06},
        {"x": 0.04, "y": 0.06}
      ],
      "sensor_size": 0.012,
      "wheel_radius": 0.03,
      "wheel_track": 0.095
    },
    "controller": {
      "base_speed": 1.4,
      "kp": 3.5,
      "ki": 0.2,
      "kd": 0.6,
      "sensor_weights": [-2, -1, 0, 1, 2],
      "line_loss": "last_direction"
    },
    "observation_request": {
      "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort", "controller_errors", "noise_robustness"]
    },
    "notes": "Final-iteration package: Builder's 0.40 kg mass on the iteration 10 row and controller. Only mass differs from the best measured design (556.7), so the race isolates the mass step.",
    "learning": "Mass 0.60 to 0.45 kg was the only recent change that improved the s-bend (score 465.8 to 556.7) without hurting the oval. This race tests whether that trend continues to the contract minimum of 0.40 kg."
  },
  "entityR1": {
    "feedback": "Written before this candidate has raced. Evidence: iteration 10 (mass 0.45 kg, five sensors at x = ±0.04 m, y 0.06 m, 12 mm footprints, wheel_track 0.095 m, kp 3.5, kd 0.6) scored 556.7, the best so far, with oval finish 1.00 and s-bend finish 0.55 with max_error 0.050 m. Iteration 5 (mass 0.6 kg, same row) scored 465.8 with s-bend finish 0.20 and oscillation 0.377. Since iteration 5, the mass cut has been the only lever that improved the s-bend without hurting the oval, and s-bend oscillation fell as mass fell. What I compensated for in the controller: nothing changed for this step. I kept kp 3.5, kd 0.6 and ki 0.2 so that the race reads mass alone. Every other sensor, footprint, forward-offset and span change tested in iterations 6 to 9 scored lower than iteration 5, so the sensor row is near its best and should stay as it is. Read the next race as follows. If s-bend max_error falls below 0.05 m and the oval finish holds at 1.00, the lighter body is helping and the score should beat 556.7. If the oval finish drops below 0.95 or s-bend steering_oscillation rises above about 0.43, lower mass is adding slip or jitter, and the design should return to 0.45 kg. The next iteration (if any) should not change the sensor row.",
    "requested_changes": [
      "Keep the five-sensor row at x = ±0.04 m, y = 0.06 m, 12 mm footprints and wheel_track 0.095 m for any further mass test. Evidence: iteration 6 (±0.05 m) dropped the oval finish to 0.90, iteration 7 (y 0.07 m) pushed s-bend max_error to 0.0626 m, and iteration 9 (16 mm footprint) scored 373.0.",
      "If the oval finish falls below 0.95 or s-bend steering_oscillation rises above about 0.43 at 0.40 kg, return mass to 0.45 kg, the best measured value. Evidence: iteration 10 gave oval 1.00 and s-bend oscillation 0.36 at 0.45 kg.",
      "Treat any remaining s-bend off-track exits as a controller-side problem rather than a geometry change. Evidence: iteration 10 still had 9 of 20 s-bend runs leave the track, while the fixed 0.06 m off-track limit holds for every allowed body_width."
    ]
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity1": {
      "label": "Final: mass 0.40 kg, iter 10 row",
      "notes": "Keeps the best measured geometry (iteration 10 row, 556.7) and the iteration 10 controller, changing only mass to 0.40 kg as the Builder proposed. The mass trend from 0.60 to 0.45 kg improved the s-bend without hurting the oval."
    },
    "entityR1": {
      "label": "Final feedback: hold row, test mass",
      "notes": "Feedback written before the race. It cites iterations 5, 6, 7, 9 and 10 to show the sensor row is near its best and mass is the remaining lever. It gives conditional rules for reverting to 0.45 kg if the oval or s-bend oscillation worsens."
